## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP over partitioning with an order-dependent gain, plus reconstruction. Distinct from standard sum-of-maximums or cooldown problems.

## Key Concepts

- Order-dependent block weighting
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- 2D DP with backtracking pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // end a block at i
      for (let j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (pref[i+1] - pref[j]) * t;
        if (val > dp[i+1][t]) {
          dp[i+1][t] = val;
          par[i+1][t] = j;
        }
      }
      // skip i is implicit via dp[i+1][t] initialization from dp[i][t]
      if (dp[i][t] > dp[i+1][t]) {
        dp[i+1][t] = dp[i][t];
        par[i+1][t] = -2; // indicate skip
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to near O(nk) for special A (e.g., non-decreasing) or under additional constraints?
- How would you extend to allow at most k blocks or to recover the exact endpoints efficiently?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This classic problem evaluates your ability to design clean DP states for interval selection with reconstruction requirements. It strikes the right balance—accessible to beginners while effectively testing state structuring and backtracking skills.

## Key Concepts
- **dp[i][t]** manages selections across the first i elements with at most t subarrays
- **local[i][t]** distinguishes between extending an existing subarray and starting a new one
- Backtracking through the DP table to reconstruct segment boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSubarrays(nums, k) {
    if (!nums.length || k <= 0) return { maxSum: 0, subarrays: [] };

    const n = nums.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const endIdx = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
            local[i][t] = Math.max(
                local[i - 1][t] + nums[i - 1],
                dp[i - 1][t - 1] + nums[i - 1]
            );
            if (local[i][t] === dp[i - 1][t - 1] + nums[i - 1]) {
                endIdx[i][t] = i - 1;
            }
            dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t] >= local[i][t] ? dp[i - 1][t] : local[i][t];
        }
    }

    const subarrays = [];
    let j = n, t = k;
    while (t > 0 && j > 0) {
        if (endIdx[j][t] !== -1) {
            let start = endIdx[j][t];
            while (start > 0 && endIdx[start][t] !== -1) start--;
            subarrays.unshift([start, endIdx[j][t]]);
            j = start;
            t--;
        } else {
            break;
        }
    }

    return { maxSum: dp[n][k], subarrays };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests a practical DP pattern: partitioning a string into k+1 substrings with a sum objective and exact k cuts, including reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with exact cuts
- Substring value computation via prefix powers of 10
- Backtracking to recover split points

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const a = s.split('').map(Number);
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  const pow10 = new Array(n+1).fill(1);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){pref[i] = pref[i-1]*10 + a[i-1]; pow10[i] = pow10[i-1]*10;}
  const val = (l,r)=> pref[r]-pref[l-1]*pow10[r-l+1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][0] = val(1,i); }
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1]===-Infinity) continue;
        const cur = dp[j][t-1] + val(j+1,i);
        if(cur>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=cur; pre[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct using n and k
  let cuts=[]; let i=n, t=k; while(t>0){ const j=pre[i][t]; cuts.push(j); i=j; t--; }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], cuts};
}
```

