## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a non-adjacent removal constraint and requires reconstruction of which indices were removed.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP state dp[i][t][r]
- Non-adjacent constraint during removal
- Path reconstruction for the chosen indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxRemainingSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY / 4;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: k + 1 }, () => [NEG, NEG])
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // keep i
      dp[i][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t][0], dp[i-1][t][1]) + A[i-1];
      // remove i
      if (t > 0) dp[i][t][1] = dp[i-1][t-1][0];
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n][k][0], dp[n][k][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to reduce space to O(k)?
- How to modify for at-most-k removals?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive a multi-interval DP with per-interval cost and reconstruction. Requires careful state definition and an O(nk) optimization akin to advanced interval DP.

## Key Concepts

- DP over prefixes and interval counts
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running maximum optimization to avoid O(n^2k)
- Path reconstruction to recover subarray boundaries

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    let best = NEG;
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - S[i-1] + c*(i-1);
      if (val > best) { best = val; }
      const cand = S[i] - c*i + best;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; prv[i][t] = i; }
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; prv[i][t] = -1; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct (simple backtrack)
  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prv[i][t] === -1) { i--; continue; }
    const end = i;
    const start = prv[i][t];
    intervals.push([start, end]);
    i = start - 1; t--;
  }
  intervals.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], intervals };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to allow variable per-interval cost or rewards based on length?
- How would you optimize memory to O(nk) and still reconstruct intervals?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces a grid-path DP with a bounded number of teleports, forcing state compression and a 2D prefix max to keep complexity at O(KRC). It tests correctness, backtracking, and memory considerations beyond standard path DP.

## Key Concepts
- DP on grid with at most k teleports
- 2D prefix max to propagate bestPrev for teleport transitions
- Reconstruction: store choice or re-run DP with pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxEnergy(grid, k) {
  const R = grid.length, C = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  let dpPrev = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
  dpPrev[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    const bestPrev = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
    for (let r = 0; r < R; r++) {
      for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) {
        bestPrev[r][c] = dpPrev[r][c];
        if (r > 0) bestPrev[r][c] = Math.max(bestPrev[r][c], bestPrev[r-1][c]);
        if (c > 0) bestPrev[r][c] = Math.max(bestPrev[r][c], bestPrev[r][c-1]);
      }
    }
    const dpCurr = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
    for (let r = 0; r < R; r++) {
      for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) {
        if (r === 0 && c === 0) continue;
        let best = NEG;
        if (r > 0) best = Math.max(best, dpCurr[r-1][c]);
        if (c > 0) best = Math.max(best, dpCurr[r][c-1]);
        if (bestPrev[r][c] !== NEG) best = Math.max(best, bestPrev[r][c]);
        if (best !== NEG) dpCurr[r][c] = best + grid[r][c];
      }
    }
    dpPrev = dpCurr;
  }
  return dpPrev[R-1][C-1];
}
```

